rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# Range 11: the second half of TYPE/COPY (finishing a file, the copy loop and its buffer
# helpers), the DATE and TIME commands with their number and separator parsers, and the FORMAT,
# ISDOS, LOAD, PAUSE, RAMDISK and VAR commands.
#
# The command handlers are entered from $2C57 (JP (HL)) with A = $FF (NZ), HL = the parameter
# text (ended by 0, its length at HL-1), IX = DE = the command's request block (IY+$FD) and
# the return address $2D41 on the stack.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYPE/COPY: finishing a file

@363F COPY_SET_ATTR
  The destination file is written and STOP was not pressed. When the source is a disk file
  too, gives the destination the source's attributes plus bit 5 ($20) and a time and date
  (COPY_TIME_DATE), stores them in its directory entry (function 11) and writes the drive's
  buffers back (function 14). Then COPY_FREE_SEGS.
  .363F Source (IX-$60) a disk file?
  .3643 No: nothing to copy over
  .3645 A = the source's attributes (descriptor +$13)
  .3648 Plus bit 5
  .364A The destination's attributes
  .364D Keep the destination's descriptor (IX+$30)
  .3652 DE = descriptor +$21, the date's high byte
  .3653 From the destination's own descriptor (-5)
  .3656 Destination /T switch?
  .365A Yes: keep the destination's time and date
  .365C Else from the source's descriptor (-$65)

@365F COPY_TIME_DATE
  Copies four bytes down into the destination's descriptor +$1E-$21 (where function 11 takes
  the time and the date): +$1B-$1C to +$20-$21 and +$1D-$1E to +$1E-$1F of the descriptor HL
  points into. Then function 11 (FN11_SET_ATTR) and, when it succeeded, function 14
  (FN14_FLUSH) for the destination's drive with C = 0.
  .365F HL = descriptor +$1C (source or destination)
  .3661 +$1C, +$1B to the destination's +$21, +$20
  .3665 HL = descriptor +$1E
  .3668 +$1E, +$1D to the destination's +$1F, +$1E
  .366C DE = the destination's descriptor
  .366D HL = the destination's request block
  .3670 Function 11: attributes, time, date to the entry
  .3675 B = the destination's drive
  .3678 C = 0 after a success: keep the buffers
  .3679 Function 14: write the drive's buffers back
  .367B Only when function 11 succeeded

@3680 COPY_CHAN_END
  The destination is a channel. With the destination's /A switch, and when STOP was not
  pressed, ends the text with an end-of-file mark ($1A) written with EXOS 7.
  .3680 Destination /A switch?
  .3686 A = the status kept at $35FD
  .3688 STOP pressed: no end mark
  .368A A = the destination's channel
  .368D B = $1A, the end-of-file mark
  .368F EXOS 7: write it
  .3691 Status, STOP check

@3694 COPY_CLOSE_OUT
  Closes the channel the command opened for the destination (its number at IY-$2D, $FF for
  none) through $326F.
  .3694 IY-$2C - 1: the destination's channel

@3699 COPY_FREE_SEGS
  Frees the copy buffer: the four segment numbers from EXOS 24 (the list pushed at $35AF),
  one by one with EXOS 25. The status kept at $35FD stays on the stack.
  .3699 HL = the status (AF) kept at $35FD
  .369A HL = the segment list; the status back on the stack
  .369B Four segments

@369D COPY_FREE_LP
  One pass of the freeing loop. On the third pass (B = 2), when B' is not 0 (a scratch sector
  buffer was taken instead, its address in BC'), frees that buffer with function 30 (B = 0)
  and leaves the loop.
  .369D Keep the counter
  .369F Third pass?
  .36A3 And a scratch buffer taken (B' not 0)?
  .36A8 Drop the counter
  .36A9 B = 0: free the scratch buffer
  .36AB Function 30
  .36B0 And stop

@36B2 COPY_FREE_ONE
  Frees the next segment of the list with EXOS 25.
  .36B2 C = the segment
  .36B4 EXOS 25: free it
  .36B6 The counter

@36B9 COPY_NEXT_FILE
  The file is done: the status of the copy (kept in F') returns when it is an error. TYPE
  ends the text with CR LF. When the source name had wildcards (bit 5 of its parse flags)
  the next matching file is searched for ($3CD4) and copied from $357E.
  .36B9 Drop the status kept at $35FD
  .36BA A, F = the copy's status
  .36BB Error or STOP: return it
  .36BC TYPE (not COPY)?
  .36C0 Yes: CR LF after the text
  .36C3 IX = the source's block
  .36C8 The source name had wildcards?
  .36CC No: done (A = 0, Z)
  .36CD Search for the next matching file
  .36D0 Found: copy it too

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYPE/COPY: the copy loop of $36E2

@36F6 COPY_NO_EOF
  A = 0, Z: no end of file seen yet; starts the block loop.

@36F7 COPY_BLOCK_LP
  Fills the buffer from the source and writes it to the destination, block after block,
  until the source ends. F' carries the end-of-file mark (NZ once seen). A disk-file source
  is read with function 15 (COPY_FN_BUFFER), up to the buffer's size (HL').
  .36F7 F' = the end-of-file mark (NZ: seen)
  .36F8 Source a disk file?
  .36FE BC = HL', the buffer's size
  .3702 Function 15: read into the buffer
  .3707 Error
  .3708 BC = the bytes read

@370A COPY_READ_CHAN
  The source is a channel. Without its /A switch and with a disk file as destination, reads
  blocks with EXOS 6 (COPY_FILL_BUF with A = $80); else one character at a time.
  .370A Source /A switch?
  .370E Yes: a character at a time
  .3710 Destination a disk file?
  .3714 No: a character at a time
  .3716 Read blocks with EXOS 6
  .371B Error
  .371C BC = the bytes read

@371E COPY_READ_CHAR
  Reads one character from the source channel (EXOS 5) into the buffer: BC = 1.
  .371E Buffer (BC') paged in, A = the channel
  .3721 HL = the buffer
  .3722 EXOS 5: read a character
  .3724 Into the buffer
  .3725 Status: .EOF marks the end, STOP stops
  .3728 Error
  .3729 One byte

@372C COPY_WRITE
  Writes the BC bytes in the buffer to the destination (IX now its block). Nothing read: on
  to COPY_BLOCK_DONE. With /A on the source or the destination the buffer is first converted
  (COPY_FILL_BUF with A = bit 6 or 5 of IY-$2A), which can shorten it at a $1A.
  .372C IX = the destination's block
  .3731 Nothing read?
  .3736 The /A switches (source $40, destination $20)
  .373B HL' = the count, its old value on the stack
  .373D The buffer's size to the stack, HL' = the count

@373E COPY_ASCII_CALL
  Converts the buffer for /A (COPY_FILL_BUF) and puts the buffer's size back in HL'. The
  JP NZ at $1114 that names this address never jumps (A = 0 there).
  .373F /A: convert, BC = the bytes to write
  .3743 HL' = the buffer's size again
  .3745 Destination a disk file?
  .3749 No: write to the channel
  .374C Destination file not created yet (bit 7)?
  .3750 Created: write the block
  .3753 Keep the count

@3754 COPY_CREATE_DEST
  Creates the destination file before its first block. Saves the destination's 13 name bytes
  (+8 to +$14) at IY+$6F, then puts the source's character (from the source's descriptor)
  in place of every "?" of the destination's 11-byte name.
  .3754 HL = the output buffer, IY+$6E
  .3758 DE = IY+$6F
  .3759 Kept for the restore
  .375A HL = IY+$165, the destination's name (+8)
  .375E Twice: for the restore and the fill
  .3760 13 bytes: the name and its flags
  .3763 Saved at IY+$6F
  .3765 DE = the destination's name
  .3766 HL = the source's descriptor name (+$38)
  .376A 11 characters

@376C COPY_WILD_LP
  A "?" in the destination's name takes the source's character.
  .376C A "?"?
  .3771 Yes: the source's character

@3773 COPY_WILD_NEXT
  Next character; then BC = the descriptor, DE = the request block, HL = DE' for function 6,
  and bit 1 of IY-$37 set when the source is a disk file.
  .3775 All 11?
  .3777 BC = IX+$30, DE = IX
  .377D HL = DE'
  .377E Source a disk file?
  .3784 Yes: bit 1 of IY-$37

@3788 COPY_MAKE_ENTRY
  Creates the destination's directory entry with function 6 (DIR_CREATE_ENT); only the first
  file of the command sets bit 6 of IY-$2B and takes this path. When the name is a directory
  ($98, "Directory exists"), no name was given and the source is a disk file, the destination
  becomes that directory: its request block is copied to IX+$60, request and descriptor are
  swapped with the name made all "?" (DESC_SWAP_WILD), and the name is built again from the
  source's ($3754).
  .3788 Function 6: find or create the entry
  .378A Not the first file?
  .378E Mark: the first file is done
  .3792 Not the first: create without the checks
  .3794 Function 6
  .3797 Made: open it
  .3799 $98 ("Directory exists")?
  .379D No name given (wildcards filled)?
  .37A3 Source a disk file?
  .37A9 HL = IX+$60
  .37AE DE' = IX+$60: HL for function 6 next time
  .37B1 HL = the request block, DE = IX+$60
  .37B2 48 bytes
  .37B5 Keep the destination's request at IX+$60
  .37B7 The directory found becomes the request
  .37BA The saved parse flags, ORed with B
  .37BD B = $3CBA's high byte of IX+$30 (bit 7 set)
  .37C1 And the saved flags at +$14
  .37C7 DE = the name's save area again
  .37C8 HL = the destination's name
  .37C9 Fill in the source's name again

@37CB COPY_MAKE_AGAIN
  A later file of the command: function 6 straight away.
  .37CB Function 6
  .37CE Error: restore the name and return it

@37D0 COPY_OPEN_ENTRY
  Opens the new entry with function 12 for DE + $30.
  .37D0 DE + $30
  .37D5 Function 12

@37DA COPY_NAME_BACK
  Puts the destination's 13 name bytes saved at IY+$6F back (the "?" pattern for the next
  file) and returns an error; else the block is written (COPY_WRITE_FILE).
  .37DA DE = the destination's name
  .37DB HL = the saved copy at IY+$6F
  .37DF Restore it
  .37E1 BC = the count
  .37E2 Error?

@37E4 COPY_WRITE_FILE
  The destination file exists now: writes the block with function 16 ($3862).
  .37E4 Created
  .37E8 Function 16: write BC bytes from the buffer
  .37EB Error
  .37EC Next block

@37EE COPY_WRITE_CHAN
  The destination is a channel: writes the BC bytes from the buffer (BC') in parts of at
  most 64 bytes.
  .37EE HL = the bytes left
  .37F0 DE = BC', the buffer

@37F4 COPY_CHAN_LP
  Writes the next part: 64 bytes, or the rest when fewer are left.
  .37F4 All written?
  .37F8 Keep the address
  .37F9 Page the part in; A = the channel
  .37FC BC = 64
  .37FE Next part's address on the stack
  .3801 Bytes left - 64
  .3803 64 or more left: a full part
  .3805 Fewer: BC = the rest
  .3808 And nothing left after it

@380B COPY_CHAN_PART
  Writes BC bytes from DE to the channel: one byte with EXOS 7, more with EXOS 8 (at $381A,
  inside the LD BC at $3819).
  .380B Only one byte?
  .380F A = the destination's channel
  .3812 More: EXOS 8 at $381A
  .3815 B = the byte
  .3817 EXOS 7: write the character
  .3819 Hides RST 30h / EXOS 8 (write block) at $381A
  .381C DE = the next part
  .381D Status, STOP check
  .3820 Error

@3823 COPY_BLOCK_DONE
  Back to the source's block. With no end of file seen (F' Z) the next block; else the
  file is done, A = 0.
  .3826 IX = the source's block
  .3828 The end-of-file mark
  .3829 Not seen: next block
  .382C Done: A = 0, Z

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TYPE/COPY: buffer helpers

@3864 COPY_FN_BUFFER
  Calls EXDOS function A on a file's block with the copy buffer: DE = its descriptor
  (IX+$30), BC = the count, IX = the buffer (BC'), whose address IY-$80 = 3 says is in the
  page map at IY-$36. Entered at $3862 with A = 16 (write); $3702 uses 15 (read). Then goes
  on as COPY_STATUS.
  Entry: A = the function; BC = the byte count; IX = the file's block; BC' = the buffer; IY.
  Exit: as COPY_STATUS (A = 0, Z; F' NZ on .EOF); BC, DE, HL as the function leaves them.
  Error: A = the status, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF'.
  Keeps: IX.
  .3864 Keep the count
  .3865 BC = the descriptor
  .3868 DE = the descriptor
  .386A BC = the count
  .386C The buffer's address (BC')
  .386E IX = the buffer; the block kept
  .3870 The buffer is in the map at IY-$36
  .3874 Function A
  .3877 IX = the block again

@3879 COPY_STATUS
  Checks a status for the copy loop: a 0 status still stops for a pending STOP ($2F23); .EOF
  ($E4) becomes success with the end-of-file mark left in F' (NZ, A' = $E4).
  Entry: A = the status, flags from OR A.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; after .EOF also F' NZ, A' = $E4.
  Error: A = the status, NZ: $E5 (.STOP) when STOP was pressed.
  Changes: AF, AF'; FLAG_SOFT_IRQ ($BFF2) cleared for STOP.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .3879 0 becomes $E5 when STOP is pending
  .387D .EOF?
  .3880 NZ: the end-of-file mark
  .3881 Into F'
  .3882 Success

@3888 COPY_PAGE_BUF
  Pages in the copy buffer at DE: turns the address into a segment and a page-1 address
  through the map at IY-$36 (ADDR_TO_PAGE1 with A = 3) and puts the segment in page 1.
  Entered at $3884 with DE = BC'.
  Entry: DE = the address in the IY-$36 map; IX = the channel's block.
  Exit: page 1 = its segment; D moved into $4000-$7FFF; A = the channel (IX+2); B = 0; Z.
  Changes: AF, B, D; page 1.
  Keeps: C, E, HL, IX, IY.
  .3888 Map 3: the segments at IY-$36
  .388D Into page 1
  .3891 A = the channel

@3895 COPY_FILL_BUF
  Fills or converts the copy buffer (BC') for HL' bytes, in parts of at most 64 bytes.
  1. A bit 7 set: reads each part from the channel IX+2 with EXOS 6 ($38F2, inside the LD DE at
     $38F1) until HL' bytes are read or the channel ends.
  2. Bit 7 clear (an /A switch): converts the bytes already there. A $1A ends the data (F' NZ,
     A' = $E4); TYPE also keeps CR, LF and TAB, clears bit 7 of the other bytes and shows a
     control code as "."; COPY copies them unchanged.
  Entry: A = the mode; HL' = the byte count; BC' = the buffer; IX = the channel's block; IY.
  Exit: A = 0; BC = the bytes in the buffer; F' NZ, A' = $E4 when the data ended.
  Error: A = the status, NZ ($E5 .STOP, or EXOS 6's).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF'; page 1.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3896 Keep DE'
  .3897 D' = the mode
  .3898 The buffer (BC')
  .389A DE = the buffer
  .389B HL = the bytes done
  .389E F' carry: more to do

@38A0 COPY_FILL_LP
  The next part: stops when the count is reached; else takes 64 bytes or the rest.
  .38A0 Keep the part's address
  .38A2 Page it in; A = the channel
  .38A5 Keep the bytes done
  .38A6 BC = the bytes done
  .38A9 HL = the count (HL')
  .38AC Bytes left
  .38AE None: done ($3902, inside the LD BC at $3901)
  .38B3 Left - 64
  .38B5 64 or more: a full part
  .38B7 Fewer: BC = the rest

@38BA COPY_FILL_PART
  Counts the part as done and moves the address on, then reads it (mode bit 7) or converts it.
  .38BA HL = the bytes done
  .38BB Plus this part
  .38BD The next part's address
  .38C0 Read mode (D' bit 7)?
  .38C3 Yes: EXOS 6 at $38F2

@38C5 COPY_ASCII_LP
  Converts one byte; a $1A ends the data.
  .38C5 The byte
  .38C6 $1A, the end-of-file mark?
  .38CA A = $E4 (.EOF), NZ
  .38CC Into F': the data ended
  .38CD A = 0
  .38CE The bytes after it are dropped

@38D0 COPY_TYPE_FILT
  TYPE only: keeps CR, LF and TAB; other bytes lose bit 7, and one below a space becomes ".".
  .38D0 COPY?
  .38D4 Yes: copied unchanged
  .38D6 CR?
  .38DA LF?
  .38DE TAB?
  .38E2 Bit 7 off
  .38E4 A control code?
  .38E8 Yes: show "."

@38EA COPY_TYPE_STORE
  Stores the shown byte.

@38EB COPY_ASCII_NEXT
  Next byte of the part; at the end skips the EXOS 6 hidden in the LD DE at $38F1.
  .38EC One less
  .38ED All of the part?
  .38F1 Hides RST 30h / EXOS 6 (read block) at $38F2
  .38F4 Status, STOP check

@38F7 COPY_FILL_NEXT
  After a part: the bytes not read or after a $1A (BC) leave the count; loops while F' carry
  says more is to come; else BC = the bytes in the buffer, A = 0.
  .38F7 DE = the next part's address
  .38F8 Error
  .38FA Done -= the bytes not taken
  .38FC Carry off: the next F' says "stop"
  .38FD F' carry: more to do?
  .38FE Yes: the next part
  .3900 The marks back into F'
  .3901 Hides POP HL / POP DE at $3902 (count reached)
  .3904 BC = the bytes in the buffer
  .3906 Success

@3907 COPY_FILL_END
  Restores DE' and returns.
  .3908 DE' as on entry

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATE and TIME

@390B CMD_TIME
  TIME command: sets carry (time, not date) and goes on as CMD_DATE.
  Entry: as CMD_DATE.
  Exit: as CMD_DATE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.

@390C CMD_DATE
  DATE command (and TIME's, with carry): shows or sets the date or the time; F' carry says
  which (set: the time).
  1. With parameters: sets the clock from them (DT_SET).
  2. Without: writes "Current date is " (or time) and the value (PACK_TIME_DATE, shown by $316F
     for the date, $3131 for the time) and CR LF; then the prompt (DT_PROMPT) and a line from the
     keyboard. An empty answer leaves the clock; a bad value writes the error's text and asks
     again.
  Entry: carry clear for DATE, set for TIME; HL = the parameter text; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = the error, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .390C F' carry: time
  .390D Parameters given?
  .390F Yes: set from them
  .3911 "Current"
  .3916 " date" or " time"
  .3919 " is"
  .3921 HL = IY-$26
  .3925 The time and date packed at IY-$26
  .3928 HL = the date
  .3929 Time or date?
  .392B Date: write it
  .392E HL = the time's high byte
  .392F Time: write it

@3934 DT_PROMPT
  Ends the line and writes the prompt in the order of the message language (EXOS variable
  $90; 0 takes the German table).
  .3934 CR LF
  .393A Read
  .393C EXOS variable $90
  .3940 A = its value
  .3943 0 (German)?
  .3945 No: "Enter new date"
  .3947 German: "Neue"
  .394C Time or date?
  .3950 Time: no "s"
  .3952 Date: "Neues"

@3957 DT_PROMPT_DE
  The German prompt's rest: a space, the word and " eingeben" (message $43).
  .3957 A space
  .395C "Datum" or "Uhrzeit"
  .395F " eingeben"

@3966 DT_PROMPT_EN
  The English prompt: "Enter new" and " date" or " time".
  .3966 "Enter new"
  .396B " date" or " time"

@396E DT_READ_LINE
  Ends the prompt with ": " and reads a line from the keyboard ($2FE2); also a line ended by
  .EOF ($E4) is taken.
  .396E ":"
  .3973 A space
  .3976 Read a line into the output buffer
  .3979 HL = the text after its count
  .397A Read: set from it
  .397C .EOF?
  .397E Another error: return it

@397F DT_SET
  Sets the time (TIME_FROM_TEXT) or the date (DATE_FROM_TEXT) from the text; nothing but
  blanks leaves the clock. $EB (.ITIME) writes the error's text (ERR_EXPLAIN) and prompts again.
  .397F Blanks passed: the end?
  .3982 Yes: nothing to set
  .3983 HL' = the text
  .3984 Time or date?
  .3988 Date
  .398B Time
  .398E $EB (.ITIME)?
  .3990 Another status: return it
  .3991 Write the error's text
  .3994 And ask again

@3996 DATE_FROM_TEXT
  Sets the date from the text: three numbers in the order the date order byte (IY-$4F) gives,
  each 1 or 2 digits (DATE_FIELD), with separators between them (DT_SEPARATOR). The bits 5-0
  of IY-$4F hold three 2-bit codes, first in bits 5-4: 1 day, 2 month, 3 year, 0 ignored; 0
  in all of them takes $6C >> 2 (day, month, year). A year xx is stored as xx - 80 in BCD (80-99
  1980-1999, 00-79 2000-2079). A missing number keeps the clock's value. EXOS 33 sets it.
  Entry: HL' = the text (at its first character); IY.
  Exit: A = EXOS 33's status ($EB .ITIME for a bad date).
  Error: A = $EB (.ITIME), NZ: a bad separator or text after the third number.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3996 EXOS 34: C = year, D = month, E = day
  .3998 The date to BC', DE'; HL = the text
  .3999 The date order byte
  .399C Its codes to bits 7-2
  .399E Set?
  .39A0 No: day, month, year

@39A2 DATE_READ_FIELDS
  Three fields; the codes stay on the stack.
  .39A2 Three numbers

@39A7 DATE_FIELD_SEP
  A separator before the second and third numbers; a bad one gives $EB ($3A0E).
  .39A7 Separator?
  .39AA Bad: $EB, with the codes dropped

@39AC DATE_FIELD
  Reads a number and stores it where its code says: E' the day, D' the month, C' the year
  (minus 80, in BCD).
  .39AC A = the number (BCD), carry: none
  .39AF The date's registers
  .39B0 B' = the number
  .39B1 $FF when there was no number
  .39B3 The codes
  .39B4 The next code to bits 1-0
  .39B7 No number?
  .39B8 Yes: keep the field
  .39BA The code
  .39BC 0: ignored
  .39BF 1: the day ($39CC, inside the LD B at $39CB)
  .39C2 2: the month
  .39C4 3: the year
  .39C5 Less 80, in BCD
  .39C8 C' = the year since 1980
  .39C9 B' = D', for the LD D,B that follows

@39CA DATE_SET_MONTH
  D' = the number (the month; after a year, its own value again).
  .39CA D' = the month
  .39CB Hides LD E,B at $39CC: E' = the day

@39CD DATE_FIELD_NEXT
  After the third number only blanks may follow; then EXOS 33 sets the date.
  .39CD Back to the text's registers
  .39CE The next number
  .39D0 Blanks passed: the end?
  .39D3 No: $EB
  .39D5 Drop the codes
  .39D6 The date in C, D, E
  .39D7 EXOS 33: set the date

@39DA TIME_FROM_TEXT
  Sets the time from the text: hours, minutes and seconds, each 1 or 2 digits, with
  separators between them (DT_SEPARATOR); a missing one keeps the clock's value. An "A" or "P"
  (either case) may end it: 12 A gives 0, 1-11 P adds 12; 0 P or an A hour above 12 is refused.
  EXOS 31 sets it.
  Entry: HL' = the text (at its first character); IY.
  Exit: A = EXOS 31's status ($EB .ITIME for a bad time).
  Error: A = $EB (.ITIME), NZ: other text left at the end.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .39DA EXOS 32: C = hours, D = minutes, E = seconds
  .39DC The time to BC', DE'; HL = the text
  .39DD The hours
  .39E0 None: keep them
  .39E3 C' = the hours

@39E5 TIME_MINUTES
  A separator and the minutes (D').
  .39E5 Separator, then the minutes
  .39EB Bad or none: keep them
  .39EE D' = the minutes

@39F0 TIME_SECONDS
  A separator and the seconds (E').
  .39F0 Separator, then the seconds
  .39F6 Bad or none: keep them
  .39F9 E' = the seconds

@39FB TIME_SUFFIX
  After the numbers: "A" or "P" (TIME_AM_PM), the end (EXOS 31), or $EB.
  .39FB Blanks passed
  .39FE In capitals
  .3A01 "A"?
  .3A05 "P"?
  .3A09 The end?
  .3A0A The time in C, D, E
  .3A0B Yes: set it
  .3A0D Hides POP AF at $3A0E (DATE_FROM_TEXT's codes)

@3A0F DT_BAD_VALUE
  Error $EB (.ITIME): an invalid date or time.
  .3A0F Error $EB (.ITIME)
  .3A11 NZ

@3A13 TIME_AM_PM
  "A" or "P": only blanks may follow. AM: 12 becomes 0, above 12 is refused.
  .3A13 Keep the letter
  .3A14 Past it
  .3A15 Blanks passed: the end?
  .3A18 B = the letter
  .3A19 More text: $EB
  .3A1C The time's registers
  .3A1D B = 12 (BCD)
  .3A1F AM?
  .3A21 A = the hours
  .3A22 PM
  .3A24 12 AM?
  .3A25 Yes: hour 0 ($3A2A)
  .3A27 Above 12: $EB
  .3A29 Never jumps (carry); hides LD C,0 at $3A2A: 12 AM
  .3A2C Set it

@3A2E TIME_PM
  PM: 0 is refused, 1-11 get 12 added (BCD), 12 and above stay.
  .3A2E Hour 0?
  .3A31 12 or more: as it is
  .3A34 Plus 12, in BCD

@3A36 TIME_STORE_HRS
  C = the hours.

@3A37 TIME_SET
  EXOS 31 sets the time from C, D, E.
  .3A37 EXOS 31: set the time

@3A3A DT_WORD_MSG
  Writes " time" (message $3F, with F' carry) or " date" (message $40) to the output buffer;
  in German "Uhrzeit" or "Datum".
  Entry: F' carry set: time; clear: date; IY.
  Exit: as OUTBUF_MSG.
  Changes: AF, DE.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .3A3A F' carry
  .3A3E $FF for the time, 0 for the date
  .3A3F $3F " time" or $40 " date"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT

@3A44 CMD_FORMAT
  FORMAT command: formats a drive and writes an empty file system.
  1. Parses the drive ($3C96, function 1), takes the switches /1, /H and /8 (table $3A97) into
     IY-$2A and C' ($3D3B); only blanks may follow.
  2. Function 23 (FN23_OPEN_DISK) with BC = $1964 opens a query-mode "DISK:" channel (H, kept
     in IY-$2C) and returns a segment (B, paged into page 1) and an address DE: the work space
     is DE - $1964.
  3. Asks "Destroy data on drive X: (Y/N)? " ($33F6); any other answer than yes ends it.
  4. Function 29 (FN29_INIT_FS) formats drive IX+7 with B = $F8, + 4 for /H, + 2 for /8, + 1
     unless /1, and DE = the work space.
  5. When bit 0 of function 1's flags (B) is set, goes on in VOL's code at $34A1 (HL = $3A9D,
     an empty switch table) to name the volume.
  Entry: HL = the parameter text; IX = DE = the request block; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = the error, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  .3A44 Bit 3 of the request's +6
  .3A48 Parse the drive (function 1)
  .3A4B Keep its flags (B)
  .3A4C The switches /1, /H, /8
  .3A4F Take them
  .3A52 Then only blanks
  .3A57 $1964 bytes of work space
  .3A5A Function 23: query-mode "DISK:" channel
  .3A61 The channel, closed at the end
  .3A64 Its segment
  .3A65 In page 1
  .3A67 C' = the switches
  .3A6A Bit 0 out: bit 1 /1, bit 2 /8, bit 3 /H
  .3A6B $F8, +1 unless /1, +2 /8, +4 /H
  .3A6E B = the format flags
  .3A70 "Destroy data on drive "
  .3A75 The drive's letter and ":"
  .3A78 " (Y/N)? " and the answer
  .3A7B Not yes: stop
  .3A7E C = the drive
  .3A81 DE - $1964: the work space
  .3A86 Function 29: format, write the file system

@3A8B FMT_CMD_END
  Returns the status; with bit 0 of function 1's flags set names the volume through VOL's code.
  .3A8B B = function 1's flags
  .3A8C Error
  .3A8D Bit 0?
  .3A91 The end of $3A97's table: no switches

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ISDOS

@3A9E CMD_ISDOS
  ISDOS command: loads the system file "A:\IS-DOS.SYS" (the counted name at $3B03) as an EXOS
  module and passes it the rest of the command line.
  1. An "X:" at the start of the parameters names the drive; it, else IY-$56 as it was, else
     the default drive (IY-$57) goes to IY-$56.
  2. What follows must be blank or start with "/"; the text after the "/" (or nothing) is
     copied as a counted text to IY+$6F and its address stored at IY-$7F/-$7E (ISDOS_SET_TAIL).
  3. Opens the file (OPEN_FREE_CHAN, bit 5 of IY-$37 set meanwhile) and loads it with EXOS 29
     (DE = IX); a load that returns without an error gives $EF. The stored address is then
     cleared (ISDOS_CLR_TAIL).
  Entry: HL = the parameter text (its length at HL-1); IX = the request block; IY.
  Exit: A = the status ($EF after a load that returned).
  Error: A = $AE ("Invalid parameter") for text that does not start with "/", or EXOS's error.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates.
  .3A9E HL = the length byte
  .3A9F B = the length
  .3AA0 DE = the length byte
  .3AA1 "X:" first? A' = its drive
  .3AA4 A = the length
  .3AA5 B = the drive as it was
  .3AA8 No "X:"
  .3AAA A = the drive named
  .3AAC A = the length
  .3AAD Less the "X:"
  .3AAF DE past the "X:"

@3AB1 ISDOS_DRIVE
  C = the characters left; no drive: the default drive (IY-$57).
  .3AB1 C = the characters left
  .3AB2 A drive?
  .3AB6 No: the default drive

@3AB9 ISDOS_SET_DRIVE
  Stores the drive; skips blanks; the text must end or start with "/".
  .3AB9 IY-$56 = the drive
  .3ABC HL = before the text
  .3ABE Count for the blank skip
  .3ABF Blanks passed: nothing left?
  .3AC2 Yes: an empty tail
  .3AC4 "/"?
  .3AC6 $AE: "Invalid parameter"
  .3AC9 The "/" not counted
  .3ACA Past it

@3ACB ISDOS_LOAD
  Copies the tail as a counted text to IY+$6F, opens "A:\IS-DOS.SYS" and loads it.
  .3ACB HL = the length's place
  .3ACC The tail's length
  .3ACE HL = IY+$6E
  .3AD3 DE = IY+$6F
  .3AD7 The length and the text
  .3AD8 To IY+$6F
  .3ADA HL = the copy
  .3ADB Kept in HL'
  .3ADC Set while the file is opened
  .3AE0 "A:\IS-DOS.SYS"
  .3AE3 Open it; H = the channel
  .3AEA Error
  .3AEC IY-$7F/-$7E = the tail's copy
  .3AF0 BC = IX+$30, DE = IX
  .3AF3 A = the channel
  .3AF4 EXOS 29: load the module
  .3AF6 Error: keep it
  .3AF8 Returned: $EF

@3AFA ISDOS_CLR_TAIL
  HL = 0 (no carry) and stores it as the tail's address.
  .3AFA HL = 0 or $FFFF (carry)

@3AFC ISDOS_SET_TAIL
  Stores HL at IY-$7F/-$7E: the address of the counted command tail for IS-DOS (0: none).
  Entry: HL = the address; IY.
  Exit: IY-$7F/-$7E = HL.
  Changes: none.
  Keeps: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOAD, PAUSE, RAMDISK

@3B11 CMD_LOAD
  LOAD command: loads every module from a channel with EXOS 29 until EXOS answers $EC (.NOMOD,
  no more modules), which ends it with A = 0. The parameter is "#n" (an open channel) or a
  name, opened here ($3DDB; the channel kept in IY-$2C); nothing may follow it. Without a
  parameter the AND (HL) bytes of $3B3D and $3B69 lead to $9F.
  Entry: A = $FF; HL = the parameter text; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = the error, NZ: $9F ("Wrong number of parameters"), or EXOS's.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  .3B11 A parameter? (A = $FF)
  .3B12 No: $9F
  .3B14 E = the channel; carry: opened here
  .3B17 Given as "#n"
  .3B19 Opened: closed at the end

@3B1C LOAD_CHECK_END
  Only blanks may follow the channel.
  .3B1C L = the channel, DE = the text
  .3B1D Only blanks left?
  .3B20 No: $9F

@3B21 LOAD_MODULE_LP
  Loads the next module, with the output buffer (IY+$6E) for its header.
  .3B21 B = the channel
  .3B22 HL = IY+$6E
  .3B25 DE = the buffer
  .3B26 L = the channel
  .3B28 EXOS 29: load a module
  .3B2A Loaded: the next one
  .3B2C $EC (.NOMOD): no more?
  .3B2E Another error
  .3B2F Done

@3B31 CMD_PAUSE
  PAUSE command: writes its parameters and CR LF as ECHO does ($34E2), then "Press ENTER to
  continue... " (message $44) and waits for a line ($2FE2).
  Entry: HL = the parameter text; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z when a line ended.
  Error: A = the status, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3B31 The text and CR LF
  .3B35 "Press ENTER to continue... "
  .3B3A Wait for ENTER

@3B3D CMD_RAMDISK
  RAMDISK command: "RAMDISK n" calls EXDOS function 28 (FN28_DRIVE_E) with B = n (decimal,
  0-255); the /D switch (table $3B66) first calls it with B = 0. Without a number and without /D:
  $AE; without parameters: $9F.
  Entry: A = $FF; HL = the parameter text; IY.
  Exit: A = function 28's status.
  Error: A = $9F or $AE, NZ.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, BC', DE', HL'.
  .3B3D A parameter? (A = $FF)

@3B3E RAMDISK_ARGS
  Reads the number and the switches; only blanks may follow.
  .3B3E No: $9F
  .3B40 C = the number
  .3B43 Kept
  .3B44 C = the character after it
  .3B45 DE = the text
  .3B46 The switch /D
  .3B49 Take it; C' = the switches
  .3B4C Then only blanks
  .3B4F C = the switches
  .3B50 E = the number
  .3B51 Error
  .3B52 B = 0
  .3B53 A number?
  .3B54 The switches
  .3B55 /D to carry
  .3B56 A number: make it
  .3B58 $AE: "Invalid parameter"
  .3B5A Neither number nor /D

@3B5B RAMDISK_MAKE
  /D: function 28 with B = 0 first; then function 28 with B = the number.
  .3B5C /D: function 28 with B = 0
  .3B60 B = the number

@3B61 RAMDISK_FN28
  Calls EXDOS function 28 (FN28_DRIVE_E) with B.
  Entry: B = the RAM disk's number from the command (0 for /D); IY.
  Exit: as FUNC_DISPATCH: A = the status, flags from OR A.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX.
  .3B61 Function 28

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VAR

@3B69 CMD_VAR
  VAR command: "VAR n" writes EXOS variable n's value (in decimal, $31E6) and CR LF; "VAR n ON",
  "VAR n OFF" and "VAR n v" write 0, $FF or v to it (the counted names "ON" and "OFF" at $3BDB).
  Variable 0 and other text give $AE; no parameters $9F. EXOS_VAR_RW does the reading and the
  writing.
  Entry: A = $FF; HL = the parameter text; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z.
  Error: A = $9F, $AE or EXOS's error.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, DE', L'.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3B69 A parameter? (A = $FF)

@3B6A VAR_ARGS
  The variable's number; with nothing after it the value is read and written out.
  .3B6A $9F: "Wrong number of parameters"
  .3B6C No parameter
  .3B6D C = the variable
  .3B71 Separator?
  .3B75 Bad: $AE
  .3B77 More text: a value
  .3B79 B = 0: read
  .3B7E Error?
  .3B80 HL = the value
  .3B82 Write it in decimal
  .3B85 And CR LF

@3B88 VAR_ON_OFF
  The value: "ON" (0), "OFF" ($FF) or a number.
  .3B88 Keep the variable
  .3B89 DE = the text
  .3B8A Before it, for the compare
  .3B8B The counted names "ON" and "OFF"
  .3B8E Two characters; C = 0 for ON
  .3B91 "ON"?
  .3B94 Yes
  .3B96 Three characters; C = $FF for OFF
  .3B98 "OFF"?
  .3B9B Neither: a number

@3B9D VAR_SKIP_WORD
  Steps past the word.

@3BA0 VAR_VALUE_ARG
  A number when no word matched; only blanks may follow; then writes the variable.
  .3BA1 HL = the text
  .3BA2 No word: C = the number
  .3BA6 Separator?
  .3BAA D = the value
  .3BAB C = the variable
  .3BAC Bad: $AE
  .3BAE More text: $AE
  .3BB0 B = 1: write

@3BB2 EXOS_VAR_RW
  Reads or writes EXOS variable C with EXOS 16. While IY-$2F is not $FF, the variables in the
  list at $2FB7 (two bytes an entry, the first the variable, 0 ends it) are kept in EXDOS's own
  bytes at IY+$F0 + the entry's index instead.
  Entry: B = 0 read, 1 write; C = the variable; D = the value to write; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; D = the value.
  Error: A = $AE for variable 0 (flags Z), or EXOS 16's error.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3BB2 The variable
  .3BB4 0: $AE
  .3BB7 IY-$2F
  .3BBA $FF: always EXOS
  .3BBD DE = IY+$F0, HL = the list at $2FB7

@3BC1 VAR_COPY_FIND
  Looks for the variable in the list.
  .3BC1 This entry's variable?
  .3BC2 Yes: EXDOS's copy
  .3BC4 The next copy byte
  .3BC5 The next entry
  .3BC8 The end (0)?

@3BCB VAR_EXOS_CALL
  Not listed: EXOS 16 reads or writes it.
  .3BCC EXOS 16

@3BCF VAR_COPY_RW
  Listed: reads or writes EXDOS's copy byte.
  .3BCF HL = the copy byte
  .3BD1 Write?
  .3BD4 Read: D = the copy

@3BD5 VAR_COPY_STORE
  D to the copy byte; A = 0.

@3BD8 RET_BAD_PARAM
  A = $AE ("Invalid parameter"); the flags as the jump left them.
  .3BD8 Error $AE

@3BEA NAME_CMP_LP
  The compare step of the counted-name match at $3BE2: until a difference has set carry,
  subtracts the name's byte at HL from A (first the lengths, then each given character in
  capitals); ADD A,$FF turns any difference into carry.
  .3BEA A difference seen: skip the compare
  .3BEC Same?
  .3BED Carry when they differ

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Helpers

@3CBA DESC_SWAP_WILD
  Swaps the 48-byte request block at IX with the descriptor at IX+$30, then fills the name at
  IX+8 with eleven "?" ($3CA0).
  Entry: IX = the block.
  Exit: the halves swapped; IX+8-IX+$12 = "?"; BC = HL = IX+$30; DE = IX; A = 0, Z.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .3CBA BC = IX+$30, DE = IX
  .3CBF HL = IX+$30
  .3CC1 48 bytes

@3D79 DT_SEPARATOR
  Passes a separator between numbers: blanks, a separator character (SEP_CHAR) or both. With
  no blank the next character must be a separator or the end.
  Entry: HL = the text.
  Exit: NC; A = the next character, Z at the end; HL at it.
  Error: carry: no blank and no separator.
  Changes: AF, C, HL.
  Keeps: B, DE, IX, IY.
  .3D79 Blanks passed
  .3D7C None?
  .3D7D Then a separator is needed
  .3D7F After blanks one is optional

@3D84 SEP_CHAR
  Passes one separator, ":" or one of ",", "-", ".", "/", and the blanks after it.
  Entry: A = the character at HL.
  Exit: NC; A = the next character, Z at the end; HL at it.
  Error: carry: another character (HL kept).
  Changes: AF, C, HL.
  Keeps: B, DE, IX, IY.
  .3D84 The end?
  .3D86 ":"?
  .3D8A Below ","?
  .3D8D Above "/"?

@3DB5 BCD_2DIGITS
  Reads one or two decimal digits as a BCD byte; after a leading 0 any more zeros are passed.
  Entry: HL = the text.
  Exit: NC; A = the number in BCD; HL past it.
  Error: carry: no digit.
  Changes: AF, DE, HL.
  Keeps: BC, IX, IY.
  .3DB5 The first digit
  .3DB9 E = it
  .3DBA 0?

@3DD1 DEC_DIGIT
  Reads a decimal digit.
  Entry: HL = the text.
  Exit: NC; A = the digit's value; HL past it.
  Error: carry: not a digit (HL kept).
  Changes: AF, HL.
  Keeps: BC, DE, IX, IY.
  .3DD2 Below "0"?
  .3DD5 Above "9"?

question 3A8B Which part of the FORMAT parameters sets bit 0 of function 1's flags (B), so that FORMAT names the volume through $34A1?
question 3AF6 Does a successful load of IS-DOS.SYS ever come back to $3AF6 (A = $EF), or does the module take over?
question 37BD Is the high byte of IX+$30 ORed into the destination's flags at $37BD meant to set only bit 7, or also bit 5 (wildcards) when the address has it?
question 3B5C What does EXDOS function 28 do with B = 0 (RAMDISK /D)?

feedback wrong 373E The card lists "jump $1114" as a way in, but the JP NZ at $1114 follows XOR A and never jumps (range 4 says so); $373E is only a fall-through.
feedback wrong 3A13 The cards for $39DA-$3A2E say "jumps out of the page to $000E": the JP NC at $3A29 follows a taken JR NC and never jumps; its operand is the hidden LD C,0 at $3A2A.
feedback missing 390B The command handlers' entry (A = $FF, NZ; HL = the parameter text, length at HL-1; IX = DE = the request block) comes from $2C17-$2C57, which the cards do not say; the AND (HL) at $3B11, $3B3D and $3B69 only make sense with it.
feedback pattern 3864-3907 The copy helpers keep a flag in F' across calls (the end-of-file mark, Z/NZ, and COPY_FILL_BUF's carry); the cards list AF' as "may change" without saying which flag carries what.
feedback listing 3580-363F The cards start in the middle of TYPE/COPY ($363F); the stack layout (the status at $35FD, the segment list at $35AF) and IX (the destination's block from $360C) needed the listing of range 10's part.
